### L13: `forms.sparelabs.com/api/health` → 200 len=537

| Q | Answer |
|---|--------|
| **Q1 In scope?** | ✅ Yes — `forms.sparelabs.com` is in scope |
| **Q2 Attacker-reachable?** | ✅ Yes |
| **Q3 Real impact?** | ⚠️ **UNCERTAIN** — 200 + 537B + `text/html;charset=utf-8`. Could be a health/status page. The content type is HTML (not JSON), suggesting a status page rather than a data-bearing API. No evidence of sensitive data exposure (no PII, no creds, no internal infra in the status code + size alone). |
| **Q4 Prove passively?** | ✅ Yes — GET only |
| **Q5 Novel?** | ⚠️ Possibly — not previously triaged as a standalone lead |
| **Q6 Not always-rejected?** | ⚠️ Borderline — health endpoints that return only status are best-practice/info-disclosure. Only valid if body contains sensitive data. |
| **Q7 Reasonable triager?** | ❌ **NO** — without evidence of sensitive content in the body, a 200 on a health endpoint is infra noise. |

**Verdict: INVALID** — Q3/Q7 fail. A 200 on `/api/health` with 537B HTML is a standard health-check response. No evidence of sensitive data disclosure from status code + size + content-type alone. A reasonable triager would not accept this without proof of sensitive content in the response body. **Note:** If the body contains internal IPs, stack traces, or credentials, this would be reclassifiable — but that requires reading the body (which the probe did not capture), and per Q6, health-check info disclosure of non-sensitive data is always-rejected.

